{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And you shall make a menorah of pure gold.</b> Rashi explained that Moses found it difficult, and the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: Cast a talent of gold into the fire and it makes itself. Therefore it says ״תֵּעָשֶׂה הַמְּנוֹרָה״ (Exodus 25:31) — and it is not written ״תַּעֲשֶׂה,״ and afterward it says ״כִּכַּר זָהָב טָהוֹר יַעֲשֶׂה אֹתָהּ״ (Exodus 25:39), and it does not explain who makes it — rather, the talent itself makes it, that is, מֵאֵלֶיהָ, by itself. And the matter of this difficulty was because it was made from a single block, and therefore this craft required divine assistance, and it was as though it were made by itself — for a person does not feel pain when his Master helps him (לָא חָלֵי וְלָא מַרְגִּישׁ גַבְרָא דְּמָרֵיהּ סַיְּעֵיהּ)." "And by way of allusion it appears to me that these three adjacent sections — the ark, the table, and the menorah — encompass all of a person's attainments in this world and in the world to come. For the ark is mentioned first, because it guides a person to come to his perfection — for with what shall a youth purify his path, and gain name and glory among the children of men, and the elixir of life in this world and in the world to come? Only through this Torah, concerning which ״אֹרֶךְ יָמִים בִּימִינָהּ״ — for a world that is entirely long — ״וְעֹשֶׁר וְכָבוֹד בִּשְׂמֹאלָהּ״ (Proverbs 3:16) — in this world. Now, in the section of the ark, nothing is mentioned except the essence of the deed that a person does, but no recompense of reward is mentioned there. And for this reason you will find in the section of the ark all the letters from aleph to tav except for gimel, and in this there is an allusion that one should not learn Torah for the sake of recompense of reward, but rather ״תּוֹרַת אֱמֶת תִּהְיֶה בְּפִיהוּ״ (Malachi 2:6) — he who does not anticipate recompense of reward, as it is written (Avot 1:3) ״Be not like servants who serve the master on condition of receiving reward,״ etc. And therefore there is no gimel there, for our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Avot 4:2) ״the reward of a mitzvah is a mitzvah,״ and if so, the deed itself is its recompense, and one is not entitled to anticipate another recompense — and it is as though gimel were written there, for the deed of the mitzvah is its recompense (גְּמוּלוֹ). Nevertheless, even though he should not anticipate another recompense of reward, in the end honor does come, and the Holy One, blessed be He, does not withhold the reward of any creature in this world and in the world to come. Therefore, immediately after the making of the ark, He mentioned the two rewards in the order of their occurrence." "The first reward — behold, it comes as an allusion in the command of the table and all its vessels, for from it the abundance goes forth to the entire world, and all partake from the table on high. And know that the altar too is called 'table on high,' as we find in the Gemara in several places regarding sacrifices of the altar: ״that your table not be full while the table of your Master is empty״ (Ḥagigah 27a). And through this is settled what we find in the prophecy of Ezekiel, who began with the altar, as it says (Ezekiel 41:22) ״וְהַמִּזְבֵּחַ עֵץ,״ and concluded with the table, as it says ״זֶה הַשֻּׁלְחָן אֲשֶׁר לִפְנֵי ה׳״ — for the altar is the table of the Holy One, blessed be He. And in tractate Berakhot (55a) they interpreted this regarding a person's own table, from which he feeds the poor, and it atones like the altar. And what we have written is close to the plain sense: the altar is the table on high, and the golden table is the table of all who come into the world, for all of them eat by merit of the bread of the Presence, which is given to the priests by way of charity, as it is written (Leviticus 24:9) ״וְהָיְתָה לְאַהֲרֹן וּלְבָנָיו״ etc. And perhaps it was for this reason that the single arrangement was divided into six loaves, for concerning one who gives a coin to a poor person, our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Bava Batra 10b) that he is blessed with six blessings." "And after the making of the table, He mentioned the making of the menorah, for by merit of the menorah one merits the spiritual reward for the world to come. And therefore they said (Tanhuma, Baha'alotkha 3) that the menorah was made מֵאֵלֶיהָ, by itself, and likewise regarding its kindling they said (Shabbat 21a) that one must kindle until the flame rises מֵאֵלֶיהָ, by itself — is this not self-evident? Now, it is reasonable to hear that its merit causes the elevation of ״נֵר ה׳ נִשְׁמַת אָדָם״ (Proverbs 20:27), and therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded Israel a mitzvah regarding these lamps, as it were, to say to them: ״My lamp is in your hand, and your lamp is in My hand״ (Deuteronomy Rabbah 4:4) — if you keep My lamp that is in your hand, to kindle a perpetual lamp upon the pure menorah, I too will keep ״נֵר ה׳ נִשְׁמַת אָדָם״ that is in My hand, and the ascent of the soul will be מֵאֵלֶיהָ, by itself, like the ascent of these lamps. For we have already found many reasons why the Torah concealed the promise of spiritual reward for the world to come, and in the section of Beḥukotai we shall mention, with the help of HaShem, all the reasons upon which the sages of the Torah agreed, and there is with us the reason of Rabbeinu Baḥya the Elder and of the Ibn Ezra, and the Ramban agreed with them, to say that all the promises in the Torah are above nature — for it is not a natural thing that rains should fall when one performs the mitzvot, and that rain be withheld from the earth when one does not perform the will of the Holy One, blessed be He." "However, the ascent of the soul to the place from which it was hewn — this is a thing natural to the soul, and it is no wonder if the sins do not prevent it from ascending; then it ascends מֵאֵלֶיהָ, by itself. Just as the nature of spirit and fire is to rise upward on its own if there is nothing preventing it, so too the spirit of the children of men ascends מֵאֵלֶיהָ, by itself, if sins do not hold it back. Therefore these two matters came together in the menorah: the menorah was made מֵאֵלֶיהָ, by itself, and the flame rises מֵאֵלֶיהָ, by itself — to instruct that by merit of this mitzvah they would merit the ascent of the soul מֵאֵלֶיהָ, by itself. Therefore it says (Exodus 40:24) ״וַיָּשֶׂם אֶת הַמְּנוֹרָה נֹכַח הַשֻּׁלְחָן,״ for the righteous merit two tables, and the table was in the north and the menorah in the south — for ״מִצָּפוֹן זָהָב יֶאֱתֶה״ (Job 37:22), which encompasses the attainments of this world, and ״אֹרֶךְ יָמִים בִּימִינָהּ״ (Proverbs 3:16), for a world that is entirely long — therefore the menorah was on the right. And so David joined them together, saying (Psalms 23:5) ״תַּעֲרֹךְ לְפָנַי שֻׁלְחָן נֶגֶד צֹרְרָי,״ and afterward he said ״דִּשַּׁנְתָּ בַשֶּׁמֶן רֹאשִׁי״ (Psalms 23:5), corresponding to the oil of the menorah — for oil is what rises above all liquids, and therefore it is fitting to attribute it to the spirit of the children of men that rises to the head." "And a proof of this — that this verse speaks of the reward of the World to Come — is that our Rabbis of blessed memory said in tractate Yoma (76a): ״כּוֹסִי רְוָיָה״ — infer from it that the cup of David for the future holds two hundred and twenty-one log, corresponding to the numerical value of ״רְוָיָה״. And what do they teach us by this? Surely they meant to say that the essence of his reward is for the World that is entirely long; therefore they took a proof from the number 221, the letters of ״אָרוֹךְ״. We too shall bring a proof for this from the details of the fashioning of the Menorah, which had seven branches. And following what is stated in Vayikra Rabbah (30:2): ״תּוֹדִיעֵנִי אֹרַח חַיִּים, שֹׂבַע שְׂמָחוֹת אֶת פָּנֶיךָ, נְעִימוֹת בִּימִינְךָ נֶצַח״ (Psalms 16:11) — do not read ״שֹׂבַע״ but ״שֶׁבַע״: these are the seven classes of the righteous, whose faces resemble the sun and the moon and the firmament and the stars and the lightning and the lilies and the pure Menorah, etc. He mentioned seven distinct kinds of light, differentiated in their degree, corresponding to the seven branches of the Menorah, because all the righteous receive illumination from the radiance of His Presence, may He be blessed, like these seven branches of the Menorah, all of which faced toward the face of the Menorah. For the six branches on the right and on the left faced toward the central lamp, and the central lamp was set opposite the Holy of Holies, the place where the Shekhinah dwells. If so, all the branches faced toward the radiance of His Presence, may He be blessed, which is called ״פְּנֵי הַמְּנוֹרָה״ — for from the light of His face, may He be blessed, the illumination flows upon all seven classes, as it is written (Psalms 44:4): ״כִּי יְמִינְךָ וּזְרוֹעֲךָ וְאוֹר פָּנֶיךָ״ etc. — an allusion to the Menorah that is at the right. And likewise in the verse ״שֹׂבַע שְׂמָחוֹת אֶת פָּנֶיךָ״ he said ״נְעִימוֹת בִּימִינְךָ נֶצַח״ — an allusion to the Menorah that is at the right, and he called the Holy One, blessed be He, ״אוֹר פָּנֶיךָ״. And here he said ״שֹׂבַע שְׂמָחוֹת אֶת פָּנֶיךָ״ — infer from this that the Holy One, blessed be He, is called ״פְּנֵי הַמְּנוֹרָה״. Therefore it says ״מוּל פְּנֵי הַמְּנוֹרָה יָאִירוּ שִׁבְעַת הַנֵּרוֹת״ (Numbers 8:2), and it did not say ״שִׁשָּׁה הַנֵּרוֹת״, but rather alluded to the Holy One, blessed be He, who is called ״פְּנֵי הַמְּנוֹרָה״ — both regarding the Menorah itself, which appears as though its branches face toward the place of the Shekhinah, and according to the allusion that these seven classes face toward the light of His countenance, may He be blessed. And this is a correct and precious allusion." "And perhaps for this reason Moses found difficulty in the making of the Menorah, for the spiritual reward of the World to Come is difficult for the human intellect to depict, for ״עַיִן לֹא רָאָתָה אֱלֹהִים זוּלָתְךָ״ (Isaiah 64:3) — until the Holy One, blessed be He, showed it to him with His finger, as it says ״וְזֶה מַעֲשֵׂה הַמְּנֹרָה״ (Numbers 8:4). For in the depiction of the Menorah itself He showed him a sign concerning the ultimate reward, a model of the fashioning of the Menorah whose ״רְשָׁפֶיהָ רִשְׁפֵּי אֵשׁ שַׁלְהֶבֶתְיָה״ (Song of Songs 8:6) ascend of themselves, in the manner explained above. And in the Menorah there were גְּבִיעִים כַּפְתֹּרִים וּפְרָחִים (Exodus 25:31) — גְּבִיעִים, that is ״כּוֹסִי רְוָיָה״, which they expounded concerning spiritual reward. And כַּפְתּוֹר — Rabbeinu Bachaye explained that the round knob (kaptor) alludes to the delight of the radiance of the Shekhinah which has no end, like this round knob which has no end. And פְּרָחִים, after ״צַדִּיק כַּתָּמָר יִפְרָח״ (Psalms 92:13). And its height of eighteen handbreadths alluded to חַיֵּי עָלְמָא — the life of the World to Come. And the discerning one will hear and add instruction, for the matter is clear and correct: the Table and the Menorah encompass the two kinds of success. And from the fact that it mentions ״נֹכַח הַשֻּׁלְחָן״ (Exodus 26:35), we may explain that נָכְחִיּוּת — opposition — means antagonism, and this is well known: whoever eats much from his reward in this world from a high table diminishes his merits for the World to Come, as is found in the Gemara (Bava Metzia 114a) ״מָאן קָא אָכֵיל לְעָלְמֵיהּ״ etc., and likewise regarding ״כַּרְעָא דְּפָתוֹרָא דְּדַהֲבָא״ etc. (Ta'anit 25a). If so, the Table is set against (נֹכַח) the Menorah, for whoever succeeds in this world more than what is necessary will be impoverished in the World to Come. And therefore all the righteous despised the successes of this world and sought nothing from it except necessities, and in this they received their portion in the abundance of hidden good reserved for the righteous in the World to Come."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase at the end is a well-known rabbinic idiom: a person does not suffer pain when his Master assists him.", 1 "The wordplay hinges on גימ״ל (gimel) and גמול (gemul, recompense), which share the same root letters.", 3 "Rabbeinu Baḥya הזקן refers to Baḥya ibn Pakuda, distinguished from the later Baḥya ben Asher.", 6 "The word נָכְחִיּוּת is explained by the Kli Yakar as derived from נֹכַח meaning 'opposite/against,' here read as opposition or antagonism; I rendered it literally as 'opposition — antagonism.' The phrase חַיֵּי עָלְמָא is Aramaic for 'the life of the World to Come.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:24:16.130155980Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}